From 746a98743dcf01dcac23cee539b43415e362e7c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Raza <raza@bukc.edu.pk>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 00:19:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(auth): OTP-based password reset/change, coordinator
 invite hard-delete
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two features, both explicitly requested.

1. PASSWORD RESET/CHANGE MOVE TO EMAIL OTP (GitHub-'Verify via email'-style,
   per the screenshots). This deliberately deviates from AUTH-18's literal
   wording ('reset link') — a knowing product decision, not a bug, so noting
   it plainly rather than silently diverging from the documented rule.

   New crypto primitives (tokens.ts): generateOtp() (crypto-secure 8-digit
   code) and hashOtp() — an HMAC keyed with the server secret, not the plain
   SHA-256 used for the existing 256-bit tokens. An 8-digit code has ~26.6
   bits of entropy; a DB dump alone could exhaustively hash every possible
   code in well under a second against a plain hash, so the HMAC key raises
   the bar to 'dump AND server secret', not 'dump alone'.

   Migration 018 adds purpose ('RESET' | 'CHANGE_CONFIRM') and
   failed_attempts to the existing password_reset_token table — same shape,
   reused for both flows.

   Forgot Password (AUTH-18): step 1 emails an 8-digit code instead of a
   link; step 2 takes {email, otp, newPassword} instead of {token,
   newPassword}. Same anti-enumeration (step 2 gives the identical generic
   error for an unknown email, a wrong code, or both), same rate limiting,
   same 'only the latest code works' as the link version had.

   Change Password (AUTH-17) becomes two-step: request-otp (verifies
   current password, emails a confirmation code) then confirm (applies the
   change). Still explicitly NOT an email-link flow — a logged-in user's
   current password remains the primary identity proof; the OTP is a
   step-up confirmation on top of it, not a replacement for it.

   New: guess-limiting independent of the request-issuing rate limit — 5
   wrong attempts burns the current code, same as GitHub's own pattern,
   necessary once the token became human-guessable-length instead of a
   256-bit random string.

   Client: PasswordResetScreens.tsx is now a single two-step screen (email,
   then code+password) instead of email-then-separate-link-landing-page;
   the old /reset-password?token= route now just redirects, so anyone with
   a stale bookmarked link doesn't hit a dead end. ProfileScreen's Change
   Password card does the matching two-step dance.

   Added a devOtp response field outside production, mirroring the
   existing devToken convention for coordinator invites — necessary since
   EMAIL_PROVIDER=console can't be inspected by a test client the way a
   real inbox can; verified end-to-end against a real database (wrong-OTP
   rejection, correct-OTP success, session kill, guess-limit burn) before
   writing a single test against it.

2. COORDINATOR INVITE AUDIT LOG: HARD DELETE. Confirmed nothing else in the
   schema references coordinator_invite, so a real DELETE is safe — unlike
   app_user, which needs the soft-delete dance because it's referenced
   everywhere. Deleting an invite record never touches the invited user's
   actual account, whether they accepted, are still pending, or the invite
   expired unused.

Tests: rewrote every password test to the new two-step API shape (14
tests total in that describe block now, up from 6), plus 4 new tests for
the invite hard-delete (including confirming an already-accepted
coordinator's account survives deleting their invite record, and that
delete is genuinely a hard delete — the row is gone, not soft-flagged).
Caught and fixed two bugs in my own draft tests along the way (missing
account verification before a login assertion; a wrong-guess loop that
used an invalid password format, which meant the requests were failing
schema validation before ever reaching the OTP-check logic they were
supposed to be exercising — confirmed via a standalone debug script that
called the service functions directly, bypassing HTTP/Zod, which proved
the actual service logic was correct all along).

Verified against a real local Postgres: migrations 001-018 apply
cleanly, seed runs, tsc --noEmit clean on both sides, full suite —
all 7 files, 149/149 tests — passes with zero flakes this run.
